      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to **maximize the final head height of the topmost giant** when stacking them such that each giant (except the first) stands on the shoulders of the previous one.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations:\n\n- The **first giant** stands on the ground:\n  - Shoulder at height $ A_{P_1} $\n  - Head at height $ B_{P_1} $\n\n- The **second giant** stands on the first's shoulders:\n  - Their shoulder is at $ A_{P_1} + A_{P_2} $\n  - Their head is at $ A_{P_1} + B_{P_2} $\n\n- The **third giant**:\n  - Shoulder at $ A_{P_1} + A_{P_2} + A_{P_3} $\n  - Head at $ A_{P_1} + A_{P_2} + B_{P_3} $\n\n- In general, the **head height of the last (topmost) giant** $ P_N $ is:\n  $$\n  \\text{Total height} = (A_{P_1} + A_{P_2} + \\ldots + A_{P_{N-1}}}) + B_{P_N}\n  $$\n\n  That is:\n  $$\n  \\text{Height} = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} A_{P_i} \\right) + B_{P_N}\n  $$\n\nWe want to **maximize this value** over all permutations.\n\nSo the problem reduces to:\n\n> Choose an ordering of giants such that the sum of $ A_i $'s for the first $ N-1 $ giants plus the $ B_j $ of the last giant is maximized.\n\nLet’s denote:\n$$\n\\text{Total} = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{N} A_i\\right) - A_j + B_j \\quad \\text{if giant } j \\text{ is on top}\n$$\n\nBecause we sum all $ A_i $, but subtract $ A_j $ since the top giant contributes $ B_j $, not $ A_j $, to the sum.\n\nSo for each giant $ j $, if we put it on **top**, the total head height is:\n$$\n\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{N} A_i\\right) - A_j + B_j\n$$\n\nWe just need to **maximize this** over all $ j $.\n\n---\n\n### Algorithm:\n\n1. Read all $ A_i, B_i $\n2. Compute $ \\text{total\\_A} = \\sum A_i $\n3. For each giant $ i $, compute $ \\text{candidate}_i = \\text{total\\_A} - A_i + B_i $\n4. Answer = $ \\max(\\text{candidate}_i) $\n\n---\n\n### Why is this valid?\n\nBecause no matter the order of the first $ N-1 $ giants, their total $ A_i $'s are added, and only the $ B_j $ of the last one is used instead of $ A_j $. So we can reorder arbitrarily — the sum of $ A_i $'s of the bottom $ N-1 $ is total $ A $ minus $ A_j $, and we add $ B_j $.\n\nHence, the maximum is just over all choices of who is on top.\n\n---\n\nNow implement accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    giants = []\n    total_A = 0\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        a, b = map(int, data[i].split())\n        giants.append((a, b))\n        total_A += a\n    \n    max_height = 0\n    for a, b in giants:\n        height = total_A - a + b\n        if height > max_height:\n            max_height = height\n    \n    print(max_height)\n\nsolve()\n```",
