y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given an undirected graph and told that it's **good**, meaning that for K given pairs `(x_i, y_i)`, there is **no path** between `x_i` and `y_i` in the original graph.\n\nThen we are asked Q queries: if we add an edge `(p_i, q_i)` to the graph, is the resulting graph still **good**?\n\nThat is, for **all** forbidden pairs `(x_j, y_j)`, there must still be **no path** between `x_j` and `y_j`.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations:\n\n1. The original graph is good. So initially, for each `(x_i, y_i)`, `x_i` and `y_i` are in **different connected components**.\n\n2. We can precompute the connected components of the original graph using **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)** or **DFS/BFS**.\n\n3. When we add an edge `(p, q)`:\n   - It may **merge** two connected components.\n   - After merging, some previously disconnected pairs `(x_i, y_i)` might become connected **if** `x_i` and `y_i` now end up in the same component.\n\n4. So the new graph is **not good** if adding `(p, q)` causes **any** of the forbidden pairs `(x_i, y_i)` to become connected.\n\nBut we can't simulate all K pairs for each of Q queries (could be 2e5×2e5).\n\n---\n\n### Efficient Strategy:\n\nWe can do the following:\n\n- Use **Union-Find** to compute the connected components of the original graph.\n- For each forbidden pair `(x_i, y_i)`, we know they are in **different** components. Let’s store their component IDs.\n- When we add an edge `(p, q)`, it connects the components of `p` and `q`. Let’s say they are in components `A` and `B`. If they're already connected, nothing changes.\n\nNow, after connecting `A` and `B`, any two nodes that were in `A` and `B` respectively become connected.\n\nSo, a forbidden pair `(x_i, y_i)` becomes connected **if and only if**:\n- One of `x_i`, `y_i` is in component `A` and the other in component `B`.\n\nTherefore, for each forbidden pair, we can **pre-store** the component pair `(min(comp[x], comp[y]), max(comp[x], comp[y]))` (since undirected) as a **forbidden component connection**.\n\nThen, when we add an edge `(p, q)`:\n- Find the components of `p` and `q`: say `cp = find(p)`, `cq = find(q)`\n- If `cp == cq`, nothing changes → graph remains good.\n- Else, assume `cp < cq`, then check if the pair `(cp, cq)` is in the set of **forbidden component connections**.\n  - If **yes**, then adding this edge will connect some `(x_i, y_i)` → not good → \"No\"\n  - If **no**, then no forbidden pair becomes connected → still good → \"Yes\"\n\nBut wait: could multiple forbidden pairs be involved? Yes, but we only care whether **any** becomes connected.\n\nSo we can:\n- Precompute component IDs for all nodes.\n- For each forbidden pair `(x_i, y_i)`, get `cx = find(x_i)`, `cy = find(y_i)`, normalize to `(min(cx, cy), max(cx, cy))`, and add this component pair to a **set**: `forbidden_component_pairs`.\n\nThen for each query `(p, q)`:\n- Get `cp = find(p)`, `cq = find(q)`\n- If `cp == cq`: \"Yes\" (no change)\n- Else:\n  - Let `key = (min(cp, cq), max(cp, cq))`\n  - If `key` in `forbidden_component_pairs`: \"No\"\n  - Else: \"Yes\"\n\nBut **wait**: is this correct?\n\nLet’s test with sample:\n\n```\n6 6\n1 2\n2 3\n2 3\n3 1\n5 4\n5 5\n3\n1 5\n2 6\n4 3\n4\n2 5\n2 6\n5 6\n5 4\n```\n\nStep 1: Build graph with 6 nodes, 6 edges.\n\nEdges:\n- 1-2, 2-3, 2-3 (duplicate), 3-1 → forms triangle 1-2-3\n- 5-4 → connects 5 and 4\n- 5-5 → self-loop, ignore for connectivity\n\nSo components:\n- C1: {1,2,3}\n- C2: {4,5}\n- C3: {6}\n\nLet’s assign root/representative:\n- comp[1]=comp[2]=comp[3] = 1 (say)\n- comp[4]=comp[5] = 4\n- comp[6] = 6\n\nForbidden pairs:\n1. (1,5): comp[1]=1, comp[5]=4 → component pair (1,4)\n2. (2,6): comp[2]=1, comp[6]=6 → (1,6)\n3. (4,3): comp[4]=4, comp[3]=1 → (1,4) again\n\nSo `forbidden_component_pairs = {(1,4), (1,6)}`\n\nNow queries:\n\n1. (2,5): comp[2]=1, comp[5]=4 → merge (1,4) → (1,4) in set → \"No\" ✅\n2. (2,6): comp[2]=1, comp[6]=6 → merge (1,6) → in set → \"No\" ✅\n3. (5,6): comp[5]=4, comp[6]=6 → merge (4,6) → (4,6) not in set → \"Yes\" ✅\n4. (5,4): comp[5]=4, comp[4]=4 → already connected → \"Yes\" ✅\n\nPerfect.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Plan:\n\n1. Read N, M.\n2. Initialize Union-Find (DSU) with N+1 nodes (1-indexed).\n3. For each edge (u,v), if u != v, union(u, v). (Self-loops don't affect connectivity)\n4. Read K, and for each (x,y), find root of x and y, store normalized component pair (min, max) in a set: `bad_components`.\n5. Read Q, for each (p,q):\n   - Find root of p and q.\n   - If same → \"Yes\"\n   - Else, check if (min(r1,r2), max(r1,r2)) in `bad_components` → if yes: \"No\", else \"Yes\"\n\nNote: We are not actually modifying the graph. We are only checking what **would** happen if we added the edge.\n\nWe do **not** perform union during queries.\n\n---\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nsys.setrecursionlimit(300000)\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n    n = int(next(it)); m = int(next(it))\n    \n    parent = list(range(n+1))\n    rank = [0] * (n+1)\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n    \n    # Read M edges\n    for _ in range(m):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        if u != v:  # ignore self-loop for connectivity\n            union(u, v)\n    \n    # Now compress all paths\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        find(i)\n    \n    # Read K forbidden pairs\n    k = int(next(it))\n    bad_component_pairs = set()\n    \n    for _ in range(k):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next(it))\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        # They are in different components (given)\n        if rx > ry:\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        bad_component_pairs.add((rx, ry))\n    \n    # Read Q queries\n    q = int(next(it))\n    out_lines = []\n    \n    for _ in range(q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q_node = int(next(it))\n        rp = find(p)\n        rq = find(q_node)\n        if rp == rq:\n            out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            if rp > rq:\n                rp, rq = rq, rp\n            if (rp, rq) in bad_component_pairs:\n                out_lines.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    print(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nmain()\n```\n\n---\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- We use DSU to compute connected components of the original graph.\n- We ignore self-loops (u == v) when building the graph (though they don’t affect connectivity).\n- We compress all paths at the end to ensure `find()` returns the final root.\n- For each forbidden pair, we record the unordered pair of component roots.\n- For each query, we check if adding the edge would merge two components that are \"forbidden\" to merge (i.e., any forbidden node pair spans them).\n\nThis runs in O((M + K + Q) α(N)) which is efficient for the constraints.\n\n---\n\nLet’s run the sample:\n\n- Components: {1,2,3} → root 1, {4,5} → root 4, {6} → root 6\n- Forbidden component pairs: (1,4) from (1,5) and (4,3), (1,6) from (2,6)\n- Queries:\n  - (2,5): (1,4) → in set → \"No\"\n  - (2,6): (1,6) → in set → \"No\"\n  - (5,6): (4,6) → not in set → \"Yes\"\n  - (5,4): same component → \"Yes\"\n\nOutput:\n```\nNo\nNo\nYes\nYes\n```\n\nMatches.\n\n✅ Correct.",
