    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the sum of power for all non-empty groups of heroes, where the power of a group is (max)^2 * (min).\n\nThe key insight is that instead of enumerating all possible groups (which would be 2^n), I can sort the array and use a mathematical approach to calculate the contribution of each element as the maximum and minimum in various groups.\n\nWhen I sort the array, I can consider each element's contribution when it serves as the maximum in a group. For each element at position i (0-indexed) in the sorted array:\n- It can be the maximum in any group that contains it and any subset of elements from positions 0 to i-1\n- If it's the maximum, then the minimum of the group will be the smallest element in that group\n\nFor element nums[i] in sorted order:\n- It appears as maximum in 2^i groups (choosing any subset of the i elements before it)\n- When it's the maximum, its contribution is nums[i]^2 multiplied by the sum of minimums of all groups where nums[i] is the maximum\n\nTo calculate the sum of minimums efficiently, I can use dynamic programming where I track the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of the first i elements.\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    n = len(nums)\n    result = 0\n    sum_of_mins = 0  # sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of processed elements\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # Current element nums[i] will be the maximum in new groups\n        # Each existing subset can either include or exclude nums[i]\n        # But we're interested in groups where nums[i] is the maximum\n        \n        # Contribution when nums[i] is alone: nums[i]^2 * nums[i]\n        # Contribution when nums[i] is with others: nums[i]^2 * (sum of minimums of all existing non-empty subsets)\n        # Plus nums[i] as a new singleton group\n        \n        contribution = (nums[i] * nums[i] % MOD) * (sum_of_mins + nums[i]) % MOD\n        result = (result + contribution) % MOD\n        \n        # Update sum_of_mins for next iteration\n        # Each existing subset can include nums[i], but the minimum remains the same\n        # Plus new singleton {nums[i]}\n        sum_of_mins = (sum_of_mins * 2 + nums[i]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
