    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string moves of length n consisting only of characters 'L', 'R', and '_'. The string represents your movement on a number line starting from the origin 0.\nIn the i^th move, you can choose one of the following directions:\n\nmove to the left if moves[i] = 'L' or moves[i] = '_'\nmove to the right if moves[i] = 'R' or moves[i] = '_'\n\nReturn the distance from the origin of the furthest point you can get to after n moves.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: moves = \"L_RL__R\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The furthest point we can reach from the origin 0 is point -3 through the following sequence of moves \"LLRLLLR\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: moves = \"_R__LL_\"\nOutput: 5\nExplanation: The furthest point we can reach from the origin 0 is point -5 through the following sequence of moves \"LRLLLLL\".\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: moves = \"_______\"\nOutput: 7\nExplanation: The furthest point we can reach from the origin 0 is point 7 through the following sequence of moves \"RRRRRRR\".\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= moves.length == n <= 50\nmoves consists only of characters 'L', 'R' and '_'.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef furthestDistanceFromOrigin(moves):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the furthest distance from origin after making moves.\n    \n    Args:\n        moves (str): String containing 'L', 'R', and '_' characters\n        \n    Returns:\n        int: The furthest distance from origin possible\n    \"\"\"\n    # Count the number of underscores\n    blank_count = moves.count('_')\n    \n    # Count the number of L and R moves\n    l_count = moves.count('L')\n    r_count = moves.count('R')\n    \n    # To maximize distance, we want to go in one direction as much as possible\n    # We can choose all blanks to go either left or right\n    # So we calculate both possibilities and take the maximum\n    \n    # If we make all blanks go left: total left moves = l_count + blank_count\n    # If we make all blanks go right: total right moves = r_count + blank_count\n    \n    # The final position would be (right_moves - left_moves)\n    # Distance is absolute value of final position\n    \n    # Option 1: Make all blanks go left\n    final_pos_left = r_count - (l_count + blank_count)\n    \n    # Option 2: Make all blanks go right  \n    final_pos_right = (r_count + blank_count) - l_count\n    \n    # Return the maximum distance (absolute value)\n    return max(abs(final_pos_left), abs(final_pos_right))\n```",
