    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_192",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nA single-player card game is popular in AtCoder Inc.\r\nEach card in the game has a lowercase English letter or the symbol @ written on it. There is plenty number of cards for each kind.\r\nThe game goes as follows.\n\n- Arrange the same number of cards in two rows.\n- Replace each card with @ with one of the following cards: a, t, c, o, d, e, r.\n- If the two rows of cards coincide, you win. Otherwise, you lose.\n\nTo win this game, you will do the following cheat.\n\n- Freely rearrange the cards within a row whenever you want after step 1.\n\nYou are given two strings S and T, representing the two rows you have after step 1. Determine whether it is possible to win with cheating allowed.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nS\r\nT\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is possible to win with cheating allowed, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- S and T consist of lowercase English letters and @.\n- The lengths of S and T are equal and between 1 and 2\\times 10^5, inclusive.\n\nSample Input 1\n\nch@ku@ai\r\nchoku@@i\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\r\n\nYou can replace the @s so that both rows become chokudai.\n\nSample Input 2\n\nch@kud@i\r\nakidu@ho\n\nSample Output 2\n\nYes\r\n\nYou can cheat and replace the @s so that both rows become chokudai.\n\nSample Input 3\n\naoki\r\n@ok@\n\nSample Output 3\n\nNo\r\n\nYou cannot win even with cheating.\n\nSample Input 4\n\naa\r\nbb\n\nSample Output 4\n\nNo",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    S = input().strip()\n    T = input().strip()\n    \n    # Count frequency of each character in both strings\n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    # We can rearrange cards within each row freely\n    # So we only care about the multiset (character counts)\n    count_S = Counter(S)\n    count_T = Counter(T)\n    \n    # The '@' can be replaced with 'a', 't', 'c', 'o', 'd', 'e', 'r'\n    atcoder_chars = set('atcoder')\n    \n    # Count how many @ we have in each string\n    at_S = count_S.get('@', 0)\n    at_T = count_T.get('@', 0)\n    \n    # Remove @ from the counts since they can be replaced\n    if '@' in count_S:\n        del count_S['@']\n    if '@' in count_T:\n        del count_T['@']\n    \n    # We need to make both strings have the same character counts\n    # We can use @ cards to fill in missing characters from 'atcoder'\n    \n    # Find characters that are in excess in S compared to T\n    # and characters in excess in T compared to S\n    excess_S = Counter()\n    excess_T = Counter()\n    \n    # Get all unique characters in both strings\n    all_chars = set(count_S.keys()) | set(count_T.keys())\n    \n    for char in all_chars:\n        count_s = count_S.get(char, 0)\n        count_t = count_T.get(char, 0)\n        \n        if count_s > count_t:\n            excess_S[char] = count_s - count_t\n        elif count_t > count_s:\n            excess_T[char] = count_t - count_s\n    \n    # Check if all excess characters are from 'atcoder'\n    # because only 'atcoder' characters can be produced from @\n    for char in excess_T:\n        if char not in atcoder_chars:\n            print(\"No\")\n            return\n    \n    for char in excess_S:\n        if char not in atcoder_chars:\n            print(\"No\")\n            return\n    \n    # Total @ needed to cover excess in T (to be added to S)\n    needed_for_T = sum(excess_T.values())\n    # Total @ needed to cover excess in S (to be added to T)\n    needed_for_S = sum(excess_S.values())\n    \n    # We need at_S >= needed_for_S and at_T >= needed_for_T\n    if at_S >= needed_for_S and at_T >= needed_for_T:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
