ever I encounter a character that doesn't match the current run, I need to decide whether to change it or start a new segment. But since segments must have even length, I need to be careful.\n\nActually, the optimal solution is simpler: I can use the fact that in the final beautiful string, each maximal run of identical characters must have even length. So I need to modify the string so that every run of consecutive identical characters has even length.\n\nBut that's not quite right either, because \"1100\" has two runs of length 2, which is even, but \"1001\" needs to be changed.\n\nLet me think differently. The minimum number of changes can be found by considering that in the optimal solution, we want to maximize the number of positions where adjacent characters are the same, since that allows longer segments.\n\nActually, the correct approach is to use dynamic programming or realize that we can process the string greedily by considering pairs.\n\nAfter careful analysis, the simplest solution is:\n- Process the string in consecutive pairs (0,1), (2,3), (4,5), etc.\n- For each pair, count how many 0's and 1's there are\n- The minimum changes for that pair is min(count of 0's, count of 1's) since we want to make both characters the same\n- Sum up the minimum changes across all pairs\n\nThis works because:\n1. Each pair must eventually be homogeneous (both 0's or both 1's) to potentially be part of an even-length segment\n2. The cost to make a pair homogeneous is min(number of 0's, number of 1's) in that pair\n3. Once all pairs are homogeneous, we can group consecutive identical pairs into longer even-length segments\n\n```python\ndef minChanges(s):\n    changes = 0\n    for i in range(0, len(s), 2):\n        # Look at pair (i, i+1)\n        if s[i] != s[i + 1]:\n            changes += 1\n    return changes\n```",
