    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is an infinitely long piano keyboard.\nIs there a continuous segment within this keyboard that consists of W white keys and B black keys?\n\nLet S be the string formed by infinitely repeating the string wbwbwwbwbwbw.\nIs there a substring of S that consists of W occurrences of w and B occurrences of b?\n\nWhat is a substring of S?\nA substring of S is a string that can be formed by concatenating the l-th, (l+1)-th, \\dots, r-th characters of S in this order for some two positive integers l and r (l\\leq r).\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nW B\n\nOutput\n\nIf there is a substring of S that consists of W occurrences of w and B occurrences of b, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- W and B are integers.\n- 0\\leq W,B \\leq 100\n- W+B \\geq 1\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 2\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nThe first 15 characters of S are wbwbwwbwbwbwwbw. You can take the 11-th through 15-th characters to form the string bwwbw, which is a substring consisting of three occurrences of w and two occurrences of b.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 0\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nThe only string consisting of three occurrences of w and zero occurrences of b is www, which is not a substring of S.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n92 66\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given integers `W` and `B`, and we want to determine if there exists a **substring** of the infinite string `S = \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\"` repeated infinitely, that contains **exactly `W` 'w' characters** and **exactly `B` 'b' characters**.\n\nLet’s analyze:\n\n- The base pattern is `\"wbwbwwbwbwbw\"` — let's call this `pattern`.\n- The length of `pattern` is 12.\n- `S` is this pattern repeated infinitely: `S = pattern + pattern + pattern + ...`\n\nWe are to check whether there exists **any contiguous substring** of `S` that has exactly `W` occurrences of `'w'` and `B` occurrences of `'b'`.\n\n### Key Observations:\n1. Since the string is periodic with period 12, any substring of `S` is equivalent to a substring of `pattern * 3` (or `pattern * 2` at least) when we consider wrap-around cases. Why 3? Because a substring of length up to `12 * 2` (i.e., spanning two full cycles) can be fully captured in `pattern * 3` to handle all starting positions.\n2. But note: `W + B` can be at most 200 (since both ≤ 100), and 200 is much larger than 12. So a substring of length up to 200 could wrap around many times.\n3. However, due to periodicity, the **density** of `'w'` and `'b'` is fixed. So we can use the idea:\n   - In one period (`pattern`), count the number of `'w'` and `'b'`.\n   - Then, consider whether a segment of length `n = W + B` can have exactly `W` w's and `B` b's by checking all possible starting positions in one period and simulating over multiple periods if needed.\n\nBut a better idea:\n- Since `W + B` ≤ 200, and the pattern is only 12 characters long, we can **generate a sufficiently long segment of S**, say `pattern * 20`, which gives 240 characters — enough to cover any substring of length ≤ 200 starting at any phase.\n- Then, we can check **every substring** of length `W+B` (if W+B > 0) in this extended string to see if it has exactly `W` `'w'` and `B` `'b'`.\n\nBut note: `W` and `B` can be 0, but `W+B >= 1`.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Read `W` and `B`.\n2. Let `n = W + B`.\n3. Create a string `S_long = pattern * k`, where `k` is large enough so that we can capture any substring of length `n` starting at any offset in the period. Since `n <= 200`, and period is 12, we need `S_long` to be at least `n + 11` long. So `pattern * 20` (240 chars) is safe.\n4. Slide a window of length `n` over `S_long` and count the number of `'w'` and `'b'`.\n5. If any window has exactly `W` `'w'` and `B` `'b'`, print `\"Yes\"`.\n6. If none do, print `\"No\"`.\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    W, B = map(int, input().split())\n    n = W + B\n    if n == 0:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n        return\n\n    pattern = \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\"  # length 12\n    # Repeat enough times to cover any substring of length n starting at any offset\n    # We need at least n + 11 characters to cover all starting positions in one period\n    repeats = (n + 11) // 12 + 1\n    S_long = pattern * repeats\n\n    found = False\n    for start in range(len(S_long) - n + 1):\n        substr = S_long[start:start + n]\n        count_w = substr.count('w')\n        count_b = substr.count('b')\n        if count_w == W and count_b == B:\n            found = True\n            break\n\n    print(\"Yes\" if found else \"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
