    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to understand what's being asked:\n\n1. For each subsequence of the original array, I need to count how many of its subsequences have sum equal to k\n2. Then sum up all these counts across all possible subsequences\n3. Return the result modulo 10^9 + 7\n\nLet me re-read the examples to understand the pattern. The examples seem to have some confusion in their explanations, but the core idea is:\n- We have an array nums\n- We consider all possible subsequences of nums\n- For each subsequence S, we count how many subsequences of S have sum equal to k\n- We sum all these counts\n\nUsing dynamic programming, I can track for each possible sum, how many ways we can achieve that sum, and also keep track of the contribution to the final answer.\n\nThe key insight is that when we process each number, we need to consider:\n1. How it affects the count of subsequences with various sums\n2. How it contributes to the final power sum\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # dp[sum] = number of ways to achieve this sum\n    # We'll use a dictionary or array to track counts for each possible sum\n    dp = [0] * (k + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # empty subsequence has sum 0\n    \n    total_power = 0\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        # Process in reverse to avoid using updated values\n        new_dp = dp[:]\n        \n        for current_sum in range(k + 1):\n            if dp[current_sum] > 0:\n                new_sum = current_sum + num\n                if new_sum <= k:\n                    new_dp[new_sum] = (new_dp[new_sum] + dp[current_sum]) % MOD\n                else:\n                    # If adding this number exceeds k, it can still contribute to power\n                    # when we're counting subsequences that contain this number\n                    pass\n        \n        # Update dp\n        dp = new_dp\n        \n        # The power contributed by subsequences ending with current num\n        # is the number of ways to form sum k that include this num\n        if k >= num:\n            # Add the count of ways to form (k - num) before adding current num\n            total_power = (total_power + dp[k]) % MOD\n    \n    return total_power\n```",
