    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numberOfStableArrays(zero: int, one: int, limit: int) -> int:\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # dp[i][j][0] = number of stable arrays with i zeros, j ones, ending with 0\n    # dp[i][j][1] = number of stable arrays with i zeros, j ones, ending with 1\n    dp = [[[0, 0] for _ in range(one + 1)] for _ in range(zero + 1)]\n    \n    # Base cases\n    # Arrays with only zeros (up to limit)\n    for i in range(1, min(zero, limit) + 1):\n        dp[i][0][0] = 1\n    \n    # Arrays with only ones (up to limit)\n    for j in range(1, min(one, limit) + 1):\n        dp[0][j][1] = 1\n    \n    # Fill the dp table\n    for i in range(zero + 1):\n        for j in range(one + 1):\n            if i == 0 and j == 0:\n                continue\n                \n            # Ending with 0\n            if i > 0:\n                # Add 0 to arrays ending with 1\n                dp[i][j][0] = dp[i-1][j][1]\n                \n                # Add 0 to arrays ending with 0 (if we don't exceed limit)\n                for k in range(1, min(i, limit)):\n                    dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] + dp[i-k][j][1]) % MOD\n            \n            # Ending with 1\n            if j > 0:\n                # Add 1 to arrays ending with 0\n                dp[i][j][1] = dp[i][j-1][0]\n                \n                # Add 1 to arrays ending with 1 (if we don't exceed limit)\n                for k in range(1, min(j, limit)):\n                    dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j][1] + dp[i][j-k][0]) % MOD\n    \n    return (dp[zero][one][0] + dp[zero][one][1]) % MOD\n```",
