      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    k = int(data[1])\n    \n    medicines = []\n    max_days = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[2 + 2*i])\n        b = int(data[2 + 2*i + 1])\n        medicines.append((a, b))\n        max_days = max(max_days, a)\n    \n    # We'll check each day starting from day 1\n    # For each medicine, it's taken from day 1 to day a_i\n    # Instead of checking every single day up to max_days (which could be too slow),\n    # we can collect all the days when the number of pills changes.\n    \n    # The number of pills changes when a medicine ends (on day a_i + 1)\n    # So we need to check day 1, and all days of the form a_i + 1\n    days_to_check = [1]\n    for a, b in medicines:\n        if a + 1 <= max_days + 1:\n            days_to_check.append(a + 1)\n    \n    # Also include all a_i values to check the day before a medicine ends\n    for a, b in medicines:\n        days_to_check.append(a)\n    \n    # Remove duplicates and sort\n    days_to_check = sorted(set(days_to_check))\n    \n    # For each day, calculate the total pills\n    for day in days_to_check:\n        if day < 1:\n            continue\n            \n        total_pills = 0\n        for a, b in medicines:\n            if day <= a:  # medicine i is still being taken\n                total_pills += b\n                \n        if total_pills <= k:\n            print(day)\n            return\n    \n    # If no day found in the range, it means we need to go beyond max(a_i)\n    # After day max(a_i), only medicines with a_i >= that day are taken\n    # But after max(a_i), no medicines are taken, so total pills = 0\n    # So if k >= 0, the answer would be max(a_i) + 1\n    last_day = max(a for a, b in medicines) + 1\n    print(last_day)\n\nsolve()\n```",
