    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_389",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given a simple undirected graph consisting of N vertices and M edges.\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, M, the i-th edge connects vertices u_i and v_i.\nAlso, for i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, vertex i is assigned a positive integer W_i, and there are A_i pieces placed on it.\nAs long as there are pieces on the graph, repeat the following operation:\n\n- First, choose and remove one piece from the graph, and let x be the vertex on which the piece was placed.\n- Choose a (possibly empty) set S of vertices adjacent to x such that \\sum_{{y \\in S}} W_y \\lt W_x, and place one piece on each vertex in S.\n\nPrint the maximum number of times the operation can be performed.\nIt can be proved that, regardless of how the operation is performed, there will be no pieces on the graph after a finite number of iterations.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nu_1 v_1\nu_2 v_2\n\\vdots\nu_M v_M\nW_1 W_2 \\ldots W_N\nA_1 A_2 \\ldots A_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- All input values are integers.\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 5000\n- 1 \\leq M \\leq \\min \\lbrace N(N-1)/2, 5000 \\rbrace\n- 1 \\leq u_i, v_i \\leq N\n- u_i \\neq v_i\n- i \\neq j \\implies \\lbrace u_i, v_i \\rbrace \\neq \\lbrace u_j, v_j \\rbrace\n- 1 \\leq W_i \\leq 5000\n- 0 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^9\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 6\n1 2\n2 3\n3 1\n3 4\n1 5\n5 6\n9 2 3 1 4 4\n1 0 0 0 0 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5\n\nIn the following explanation, let A = (A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_N) represent the numbers of pieces on the vertices.\nInitially, A = (1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1).\nConsider performing the operation as follows:\n\n- Remove one piece from vertex 1 and place one piece each on vertices 2 and 3. Now, A = (0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1).\n- Remove one piece from vertex 2. Now, A = (0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1).\n- Remove one piece from vertex 6. Now, A = (0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0).\n- Remove one piece from vertex 3 and place one piece on vertex 2. Now, A = (0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0).\n- Remove one piece from vertex 2. Now, A = (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0).\n\nIn this procedure, the operation is performed five times, which is the maximum possible number of times.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n2 1\n1 2\n1 2\n0 0\n\nSample Output 2\n\n0\n\nIn this sample input, there are no pieces on the graph from the beginning.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n10 20\n4 8\n1 10\n1 7\n5 9\n9 10\n8 10\n7 5\n1 4\n7 3\n8 7\n2 8\n5 8\n4 2\n5 1\n7 2\n8 3\n3 4\n8 9\n7 10\n2 3\n25 5 1 1 16 5 98 3 21 1\n35 39 32 11 35 37 14 29 36 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\n1380",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    # Parse first two values: N and M\n    n = int(data[0])\n    m = int(data[1])\n    \n    # Parse edges\n    edges = []\n    index = 2\n    for _ in range(m):\n        u = int(data[index]) - 1  # Convert to 0-indexed\n        v = int(data[index + 1]) - 1\n        edges.append((u, v))\n        index += 2\n    \n    # Parse weights W\n    W = list(map(int, data[index:index + n]))\n    index += n\n    \n    # Parse initial pieces A\n    A = list(map(int, data[index:index + n]))\n    \n    # Build adjacency list\n    adj = defaultdict(list)\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n    \n    # We want to process vertices in decreasing order of weight\n    # This ensures that when we process a vertex, all higher-weight neighbors have been processed\n    vertices = list(range(n))\n    vertices.sort(key=lambda x: W[x], reverse=True)\n    \n    # dp[i] = maximum number of operations that can be performed starting from vertex i\n    # (including the operation that removes a piece from vertex i and any subsequent chain reactions)\n    dp = [0] * n\n    \n    for u in vertices:\n        # For each vertex u, we can choose a subset S of neighbors such that sum(W[v] for v in S) < W[u]\n        # We want to maximize the number of operations: 1 (for removing from u) + sum(dp[v] for v in S)\n        \n        neighbors = adj[u]\n        if not neighbors:\n            dp[u] = 1  # Just remove the piece, no propagation\n            continue\n        \n        # We want to select a subset of neighbors with total weight < W[u] to maximize sum(dp[v])\n        # This is a knapsack-like problem: capacity = W[u] - 1, weight = W[v], value = dp[v]\n        capacity = W[u] - 1\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            dp[u] = 1  # Can't place on any neighbor\n            continue\n        \n        # Knapsack DP\n        knap = [0] * (capacity + 1)\n        \n        for v in neighbors:\n            weight = W[v]\n            value = dp[v]\n            if weight > capacity:\n                continue\n            # Do knapsack backwards to avoid using same item twice\n            for w in range(capacity, weight - 1, -1):\n                knap[w] = max(knap[w], knap[w - weight] + value)\n        \n        max_extra_ops = knap[capacity]\n        dp[u] = 1 + max_extra_ops\n    \n    # Total operations is sum of A[i] * dp[i] for all vertices\n    total_ops = sum(A[i] * dp[i] for i in range(n))\n    \n    print(total_ops)\n\nsolve()\n```",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
