      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input\n    H, W, M = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n    \n    # We'll track the last operation for each row and column\n    # This is because later operations override earlier ones\n    last_row_op = {}  # row -> (color, operation_index)\n    last_col_op = {}  # col -> (color, operation_index)\n    \n    operations = []\n    for i in range(M):\n        t, a, x = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n        operations.append((t, a, x))\n        if t == 1:\n            last_row_op[a] = (x, i)\n        else:  # t == 2\n            last_col_op[a] = (x, i)\n    \n    # Count cells by color\n    color_count = defaultdict(int)\n    \n    # For each cell (i, j), determine its final color\n    for i in range(1, H + 1):\n        for j in range(1, W + 1):\n            row_color = last_row_op.get(i, (0, -1))  # default color 0 if no row operation\n            col_color = last_col_op.get(j, (0, -1))  # default color 0 if no column operation\n            \n            # The operation with higher index (later) takes precedence\n            if row_color[1] > col_color[1]:\n                final_color = row_color[0]\n            else:\n                final_color = col_color[0]\n            \n            color_count[final_color] += 1\n    \n    # Output results\n    K = len(color_count)\n    print(K)\n    \n    # Sort by color number and print\n    for color in sorted(color_count.keys()):\n        print(color, color_count[color])\n\nsolve()\n```",
