      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    t = int(input())\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(input())\n        a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n        \n        if n == 1:\n            print(a[0])\n            continue\n            \n        # We need to find maximum sum subarray with alternating parities\n        # Use modified Kadane's algorithm, but restart when parity constraint is violated\n        \n        max_sum = a[0]\n        current_sum = a[0]\n        \n        for i in range(1, n):\n            # Check if current and previous have different parities\n            has_alternating_parity = (a[i] % 2) != (a[i-1] % 2)\n            \n            if has_alternating_parity:\n                # We can extend the current subarray\n                if current_sum > 0:\n                    current_sum += a[i]\n                else:\n                    current_sum = a[i]\n            else:\n                # Parity constraint violated, must start new subarray\n                # But we can also consider starting fresh from current element\n                current_sum = a[i]\n            \n            # Also consider starting a new subarray from current element\n            # This handles cases where it's better to start fresh\n            max_sum = max(max_sum, current_sum)\n            \n            # Additionally, we need to consider all possible alternating sequences\n            # So we'll also check if we should restart even with alternating parity\n            if current_sum < a[i]:\n                current_sum = a[i]\n        \n        # The above logic might miss some cases, so let's use a different approach\n        # Try all possible starting points and extend as long as alternating\n        max_sum = a[0]\n        \n        for start in range(n):\n            current_sum = a[start]\n            max_sum = max(max_sum, current_sum)\n            \n            for end in range(start + 1, n):\n                # Check if we can include a[end] - need alternating parity with a[end-1]\n                if (a[end] % 2) != (a[end-1] % 2):\n                    current_sum += a[end]\n                    max_sum = max(max_sum, current_sum)\n                else:\n                    break  # Can't extend further with alternating parity\n        \n        print(max_sum)\n\nsolve()\n```",
