      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    t = int(input())\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n, k = map(int, input().split())\n        a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n        \n        # If the current product is already divisible by k, no operations needed\n        current_product = 1\n        for num in a:\n            current_product *= num\n            # We can take modulo k to avoid overflow since we only care about divisibility\n            current_product %= k\n        \n        if current_product % k == 0:\n            print(0)\n            continue\n        \n        # Try all possible distributions of operations across indices\n        # Since k is small (2 to 5) and a[i] is at most 10, we don't need too many operations\n        # We'll try to find the minimum operations needed\n        \n        min_operations = float('inf')\n        \n        # We'll try up to 10 operations total (more than enough given constraints)\n        # Use BFS-like approach for small state space\n        from collections import deque\n        \n        # State: (operations_used, current_values_tuple)\n        # But instead of tracking full state, we can be smarter\n        \n        # Since n and k are small, and values are small, we can try brute force\n        # For each position, we can try adding 0, 1, 2, ... up to some limit\n        # But better: try all combinations of small increments\n        \n        # We'll try all possible ways to distribute up to 10 operations\n        # But since k is very small (2-5), we can use simpler logic\n        \n        # Alternative approach: try 0 to k-1 operations on each element, but smartly\n        \n        # Since k is at most 5, and we only care about product mod k,\n        # we can try all possible number of operations from 0 upwards until we find solution\n        \n        found = False\n        for ops in range(11):  # try up to 10 operations\n            if found:\n                break\n            # Try all ways to distribute 'ops' operations among n positions\n            # This is equivalent to integer composition\n            from itertools import combinations_with_replacement\n            \n            # Generate all ways to distribute 'ops' operations into n bins\n            # Each way is a tuple of length n indicating how many operations on each index\n            if ops == 0:\n                distributions = [[0] * n]\n            else:\n                distributions = []\n                # Generate compositions of ops into n parts\n                from itertools import combinations\n                # Stars and bars: C(ops + n - 1, n - 1)\n                for c in combinations(range(ops + n - 1), n - 1):\n                    dist = []\n                    prev = 0\n                    for i, gap in enumerate(c):\n                        dist.append(gap - prev - i)\n                        prev = gap\n                    dist.append(ops + n - 1 - prev - (n - 1))\n                    distributions.append(dist)\n            \n            for dist in distributions:\n                new_a = [a[i] + dist[i] for i in range(n)]\n                product = 1\n                for num in new_a:\n                    product *= num\n                    product %= k  # avoid overflow\n                \n                if product % k == 0:\n                    min_operations = ops\n                    found = True\n                    break\n        \n        print(min_operations)\n\nsolve()\n```",
