      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to determine if two numbers A and B (with A < B) written on a 3×3 board are **adjacent horizontally**.\n\n### Step 1: Understand the board layout\nThe 3×3 board contains numbers 1 through 9 arranged as follows:\n\n```\n1 2 3\n4 5 6\n7 8 9\n```\n\nSo the positions are:\n\n- Row 0: [1, 2, 3]\n- Row 1: [4, 5, 6]\n- Row 2: [7, 8, 9]\n\n### Step 2: Define horizontal adjacency\nTwo numbers are horizontally adjacent if they are in the **same row** and their positions are **next to each other**.\n\nSo possible horizontally adjacent pairs are:\n- Row 0: (1,2), (2,3)\n- Row 1: (4,5), (5,6)\n- Row 2: (7,8), (8,9)\n\nSo the valid (A, B) pairs where A < B and they are horizontally adjacent are:\n- (1,2), (2,3), (4,5), (5,6), (7,8), (8,9)\n\n### Step 3: Algorithm\n- Read A and B from stdin.\n- Check if (A, B) is one of the valid adjacent pairs listed above.\n- Print \"Yes\" if it is, \"No\" otherwise.\n\n### Step 4: Implementation\n\nWe can hardcode the valid pairs or compute them logically. Since the board is small and fixed, we can just check against the known valid pairs.\n\nLet’s write the function.\n\n```python\n# Read input\nA, B = map(int, input().split())\n\n# Define the set of valid horizontally adjacent pairs (A, B) with A < B\nvalid_pairs = {(1,2), (2,3), (4,5), (5,6), (7,8), (8,9)}\n\n# Check if (A, B) is in the valid pairs\nif (A, B) in valid_pairs:\n    print(\"Yes\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No\")\n```\n\nThis will work for all test cases.\n\nLet’s test with the samples:\n- Sample 1: (7,8) → in valid_pairs → \"Yes\" ✅\n- Sample 2: (1,9) → not in valid_pairs → \"No\" ✅\n- Sample 3: (3,4) → not in valid_pairs → \"No\" ✅\n\nPerfect.\n\nNow, we just need to make sure the code is executable and takes stdin as input.\n\nHere is the final code:\n\n```python\nA, B = map(int, input().split())\nvalid_pairs = {(1,2), (2,3), (4,5), (5,6), (7,8), (8,9)}\nprint(\"Yes\" if (A, B) in valid_pairs else \"No\")\n```",
