    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_27",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given two integers, n and k.\nAn array of distinct positive integers is called a k-avoiding array if there does not exist any pair of distinct elements that sum to k.\nReturn the minimum possible sum of a k-avoiding array of length n.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: n = 5, k = 4\nOutput: 18\nExplanation: Consider the k-avoiding array [1,2,4,5,6], which has a sum of 18.\nIt can be proven that there is no k-avoiding array with a sum less than 18.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: n = 2, k = 6\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: We can construct the array [1,2], which has a sum of 3.\nIt can be proven that there is no k-avoiding array with a sum less than 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n, k <= 50",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum possible sum of a k-avoiding array of length n.\n\nA k-avoiding array means no two distinct elements sum to k. To minimize the sum, I should use the smallest possible positive integers, but avoid pairs that sum to k.\n\nKey insight: If I include a number `i` where `i < k`, then I cannot include `k - i` in the array. To minimize the sum, I should prefer smaller numbers. So for each pair `(i, k-i)` where `i < k-i`, I should choose `i` (the smaller one) and avoid `k-i`.\n\nSpecial cases:\n- If `k` is even, then `k/2 + k/2 = k`, but since we need distinct elements, we can include `k/2` once (it can't form a pair with itself)\n- Numbers greater than or equal to `k` are \"safe\" because when paired with any positive integer, their sum will be at least `k+1`\n\nStrategy:\n1. Start with the smallest positive integers (1, 2, 3, ...)\n2. For each number `i` from 1 upward:\n   - If `i >= k`, include it (safe)\n   - If `i < k` and we haven't included `k-i` yet, include `i`\n   - Skip `i` if `k-i` has already been included\n3. Continue until we have `n` elements\n\n```python\ndef minimumSum(n, k):\n    result = []\n    used = set()\n    \n    i = 1\n    while len(result) < n:\n        # If i >= k, it's safe to include (sum with any positive integer >= 1 will be >= k+1)\n        # If i < k, check if k-i is already used\n        if i >= k or (k - i) not in used:\n            result.append(i)\n            used.add(i)\n        i += 1\n    \n    return sum(result)\n```",
