    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_420",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given a tree with N vertices. The vertices are numbered 1 to N, and the i-th edge connects vertices A_i and B_i.\nYou are also given a sequence of positive integers C = (C_1, C_2, \\ldots ,C_N) of length N. Let d(a, b) be the number of edges between vertices a and b, and for x = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, let \\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{{i=1}}^{{N}} (C_i \\times d(x, i)). Find \\displaystyle \\min_{{1 \\leq v \\leq N}} f(v).\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\vdots\nA_{{N - 1}} B_{{N - 1}}\nC_1 C_2 \\cdots C_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer in one line.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 10^5\n- 1 \\leq A_i, B_i \\leq N\n- The given graph is a tree.\n- 1 \\leq C_i \\leq 10^9\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4\n1 2\n1 3\n2 4\n1 1 1 2\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5\n\nFor example, consider calculating f(1). We have d(1, 1) = 0, d(1, 2) = 1, d(1, 3) = 1, d(1, 4) = 2.\nThus, f(1) = 0 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 1 + 2 \\times 2 = 6.\nSimilarly, f(2) = 5, f(3) = 9, f(4) = 6. Since f(2) is the minimum, print 5.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n2\n2 1\n1 1000000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1\n\nf(2) = 1, which is the minimum.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n7\n7 3\n2 5\n2 4\n3 1\n3 6\n2 1\n2 7 6 9 3 4 6\n\nSample Output 3\n\n56",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input\n    N = int(sys.stdin.readline())\n    \n    # Build adjacency list for the tree\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    for _ in range(N - 1):\n        a, b = map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split())\n        graph[a].append(b)\n        graph[b].append(a)\n    \n    # Read the C array\n    C = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n    \n    # We'll use two DFS traversals to compute the answer efficiently\n    # First, pick an arbitrary root (1) and compute:\n    # - subtree weights (sum of C_i in each subtree)\n    # - f(1) as our starting point\n    \n    parent = {}\n    subtree_weight = [0] * (N + 1)  # sum of C_i in the subtree\n    order = []\n    \n    # First DFS to establish parent relationships and compute subtree weights\n    stack = [1]\n    parent[1] = -1\n    \n    while stack:\n        node = stack.pop()\n        order.append(node)\n        subtree_weight[node] = C[node - 1]  # C is 0-indexed, nodes are 1-indexed\n        \n        for neighbor in graph[node]:\n            if neighbor != parent[node]:\n                parent[neighbor] = node\n                stack.append(neighbor)\n    \n    # Compute subtree weights in reverse order (bottom-up)\n    for node in reversed(order):\n        for neighbor in graph[node]:\n            if neighbor != parent[node]:\n                subtree_weight[node] += subtree_weight[neighbor]\n    \n    # Calculate f(1) - the cost when vertex 1 is chosen\n    f = [0] * (N + 1)\n    visited = [False] * (N + 1)\n    queue = deque([1])\n    visited[1] = True\n    depth = 0\n    \n    # BFS to calculate f(1)\n    while queue:\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            f[1] += depth * C[node - 1]\n            for neighbor in graph[node]:\n                if not visited[neighbor]:\n                    visited[neighbor] = True\n                    queue.append(neighbor)\n        depth += 1\n    \n    # Use the recurrence relation:\n    # f(child) = f(parent) - weight(child) + (total_weight - weight(child))\n    #         = f(parent) + total_weight - 2 * weight(child)\n    \n    total_weight = sum(C)\n    \n    # Second pass to compute f(v) for all other vertices using tree DP\n    stack = [1]\n    while stack:\n        node = stack.pop()\n        for neighbor in graph[node]:\n            if neighbor != parent[node]:\n                f[neighbor] = f[node] + total_weight - 2 * subtree_weight[neighbor]\n                stack.append(neighbor)\n    \n    # Find minimum f(v) for v in [1, N]\n    answer = min(f[1:N+1])\n    print(answer)\n\nsolve()\n```",
