    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_450",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi has N cards from the card game \"AtCoder Magics.\" The i-th card will be called card i. Each card has two parameters: strength and cost. Card i has a strength of A_i and a cost of C_i.\nHe does not like weak cards, so he will discard them. Specifically, he will repeat the following operation until it can no longer be performed:\n\n- Choose two cards x and y such that A_x > A_y and C_x < C_y. Discard card y.\n\nIt can be proved that the set of remaining cards when the operations can no longer be performed is uniquely determined. Find this set of cards.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nA_1 C_1\nA_2 C_2\n\\vdots\nA_N C_N\n\nOutput\n\nLet there be m remaining cards, cards i_1, i_2, \\dots, i_m, in ascending order. Print these in the following format:\nm\ni_1 i_2 \\cdots i_m\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq A_i, C_i \\leq 10^9\n- A_1, A_2, \\dots ,A_N are all distinct.\n- C_1, C_2, \\dots ,C_N are all distinct.\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n2 4\n1 1\n3 2\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\n2 3\n\nFocusing on cards 1 and 3, we have A_1 < A_3 and C_1 > C_3, so card 1 can be discarded.\nNo further operations can be performed. At this point, cards 2 and 3 remain, so print them.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5\n1 1\n10 2\n100 3\n1000 4\n10000 5\n\nSample Output 2\n\n5\n1 2 3 4 5\n\nIn this case, no cards can be discarded.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n6\n32 101\n65 78\n2 29\n46 55\n103 130\n52 40\n\nSample Output 3\n\n4\n2 3 5 6",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given `N` cards, each with strength `A_i` and cost `C_i`.  \nTakahashi repeatedly discards a card `y` if there exists another card `x` such that:\n\n- `A_x > A_y` and  \n- `C_x < C_y`\n\nThat is, if there's a stronger card that's cheaper, then the weaker, more expensive card is considered \"weak\" and gets discarded.\n\nThe key insight:\n> A card `i` survives **if and only if** there is **no other card `j`** such that `A_j > A_i` and `C_j < C_i`.\n\nThis is equivalent to saying:  \nCard `i` remains iff for all cards `j` with `A_j > A_i`, we have `C_j >= C_i`.\n\nWe can rephrase the problem in terms of **Pareto optimality**:\n- We want the cards that are **not dominated** by any other card, where domination means:\n  - Another card has **higher strength AND lower cost**.\n\nSo this is like finding the **Pareto frontier** in 2D (maximize strength, minimize cost).\n\n### Strategy:\n1. Sort the cards by strength `A_i` in increasing order.\n2. Traverse from highest strength to lowest, maintaining the **minimum cost seen so far** (from higher strength cards).\n3. For each card, if its cost is **less than or equal to all costs of cards with higher strength**, it survives.\n\nWhy?\n- A card is discarded only if **some stronger card has lower cost**.\n- So if a card has **cost ≤ all stronger cards' costs**, then no stronger card is cheaper → it survives.\n\n### Steps:\n- Store (A_i, C_i, index)\n- Sort by A_i ascending\n- Traverse in reverse (from strongest to weakest), keeping track of the minimum cost seen\n- If current card’s cost ≤ current minimum cost, it survives (and update minimum)\n\nThen output the surviving indices in ascending order.\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    cards = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[index])\n        c = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        cards.append((a, c, i + 1))  # (strength, cost, original_index)\n    \n    # Sort by strength ascending\n    cards.sort()\n    \n    surviving_indices = []\n    min_cost_from_higher = float('inf')\n    \n    # Traverse from highest strength to lowest\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        a, c, idx = cards[i]\n        # If this card's cost is <= any stronger card's cost (i.e., <= min_cost_from_higher)\n        # then no stronger card is cheaper → it survives\n        if c <= min_cost_from_higher:\n            surviving_indices.append(idx)\n            min_cost_from_higher = c\n    \n    # Sort the surviving indices (they are collected in descending strength, but we need ascending index order)\n    surviving_indices.sort()\n    \n    # Output\n    print(len(surviving_indices))\n    print(' '.join(map(str, surviving_indices)))\n\nsolve()\n```",
