    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_485",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array nums of length n, and a positive integer k.\nThe power of a subsequence is defined as the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in the subsequence.\nReturn the sum of powers of all subsequences of nums which have length equal to k.\nSince the answer may be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,4], k = 3\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nThere are 4 subsequences in nums which have length 3: [1,2,3], [1,3,4], [1,2,4], and [2,3,4]. The sum of powers is |2 - 3| + |3 - 4| + |2 - 1| + |3 - 4| = 4.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,2], k = 2\nOutput: 0\nExplanation:\nThe only subsequence in nums which has length 2 is [2,2]. The sum of powers is |2 - 2| = 0.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [4,3,-1], k = 2\nOutput: 10\nExplanation:\nThere are 3 subsequences in nums which have length 2: [4,3], [4,-1], and [3,-1]. The sum of powers is |4 - 3| + |4 - (-1)| + |3 - (-1)| = 10.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n2 <= n == nums.length <= 50\n-10^8 <= nums[i] <= 10^8 \n2 <= k <= n",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this step-by-step.\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\n1. We need to find all subsequences of length `k` from the array `nums`\n2. For each subsequence, calculate its \"power\" = minimum absolute difference between any two elements\n3. Return the sum of powers of all such subsequences, modulo 10^9 + 7\n\n## Key Insights\n\n1. Since we're dealing with absolute differences, sorting the array won't change the final answer but will make it easier to work with\n2. After sorting, the minimum absolute difference in any subsequence will be between two adjacent elements in the sorted order\n3. We can use dynamic programming to efficiently count subsequences and track their powers\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Sort the array first\n2. Use dynamic programming where we track:\n   - The current position in the array\n   - How many elements we've selected so far\n   - The minimum difference seen so far in the current subsequence\n3. For each element, we can either include it or exclude it\n\n## Step-by-Step Solution\n\n1. **Sort the array** - this helps us easily find minimum differences\n2. **Use DP with memoization** - state: (index, count, min_diff, last_index)\n3. **For each position**, decide whether to include the current element\n4. **When we include an element**, update the minimum difference if needed\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPowers(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    from functools import lru_cache\n    \n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def dp(pos, count, min_diff, last_pos):\n        # pos: current position in array\n        # count: number of elements selected so far\n        # min_diff: minimum difference in current subsequence\n        # last_pos: position of last selected element\n        \n        if count == k:\n            return min_diff\n        \n        if pos == n:\n            return 0\n        \n        # Option 1: Don't take current element\n        result = dp(pos + 1, count, min_diff, last_pos)\n        \n        # Option 2: Take current element\n        if count == 0:\n            # First element, no difference to calculate\n            result = (result + dp(pos + 1, count + 1, float('inf'), pos)) % MOD\n        else:\n            # Calculate new difference with last selected element\n            new_diff = abs(nums[pos] - nums[last_pos])\n            new_min_diff = min(min_diff, new_diff) if min_diff != float('inf') else new_diff\n            result = (result + dp(pos + 1, count + 1, new_min_diff, pos)) % MOD\n        \n        return result % MOD\n    \n    return dp(0, 0, float('inf'), -1)\n```",
