    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a tree with N vertices numbered 1 to N.\r\nFor each i\\ (2 \\leq i \\leq N), there is an edge connecting vertex i and vertex \\lfloor \\frac{{i}}{{2}} \\rfloor.\r\nThere are no other edges.\nIn this tree, find the number of vertices whose distance from vertex X is K.\r\nHere, the distance between two vertices u and v is defined as the number of edges in the simple path connecting vertices u and v.\nYou have T test cases to solve.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format, where \\mathrm{{test}}_i represents the i-th test case:\nT\r\n\\mathrm{{test}}_1\r\n\\mathrm{{test}}_2\r\n\\vdots\r\n\\mathrm{{test}}_T\r\n\nEach test case is given in the following format:\nN X K\n\nOutput\n\nPrint T lines.\nThe i-th line (1 \\leq i \\leq T) should contain the answer to the i-th test case as an integer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq T \\leq 10^5\n- 1\\leq N \\leq 10^{{18}}\n- 1\\leq X \\leq N\n- 0\\leq K \\leq N-1\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5\r\n10 2 0\r\n10 2 1\r\n10 2 2\r\n10 2 3\r\n10 2 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n1\r\n3\r\n4\r\n2\r\n0\r\n\nThe tree for N=10 is shown in the following figure.\n\nHere,\n\n- There is 1 vertex, 2, whose distance from vertex 2 is 0.\n- There are 3 vertices, 1,4,5, whose distance from vertex 2 is 1.\n- There are 4 vertices, 3,8,9,10, whose distance from vertex 2 is 2.\n- There are 2 vertices, 6,7, whose distance from vertex 2 is 3.\n- There are no vertices whose distance from vertex 2 is 4.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n10\r\n822981260158260522 52 20\r\n760713016476190629 2314654 57\r\n1312150450968417 1132551176249851 7\r\n1000000000000000000 1083770654 79\r\n234122432773361868 170290518806790 23\r\n536187734191890310 61862 14\r\n594688604155374934 53288633578 39\r\n1000000000000000000 120160810 78\r\n89013034180999835 14853481725739 94\r\n463213054346948152 825589 73\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1556480\r\n140703128616960\r\n8\r\n17732923532771328\r\n65536\r\n24576\r\n2147483640\r\n33776997205278720\r\n7881299347898368\r\n27021597764222976",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    t = int(data[0])\n    index = 1\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        N = int(data[index])\n        X = int(data[index+1])\n        K = int(data[index+2])\n        index += 3\n        \n        if K == 0:\n            results.append(\"1\")\n            continue\n            \n        # Function to get parent of a node\n        def parent(node):\n            return node // 2\n        \n        # Function to get children of a node\n        def children(node):\n            left = node * 2\n            right = node * 2 + 1\n            return [child for child in [left, right] if child <= N]\n        \n        # Find all nodes at distance K from X\n        from collections import deque\n        \n        # BFS to find nodes at distance K\n        # But we need to be careful with large N\n        # Instead of traversing all nodes, we can use a mathematical approach\n        \n        # We'll use BFS but with optimizations\n        if K == 0:\n            count = 1\n        else:\n            # We need to find all nodes at distance K from X\n            # We can do BFS but we need to be careful with large N\n            # Instead, we can use a more mathematical approach\n            \n            # Let's think about the structure\n            # This is a complete binary tree (almost) with parent-child relationship defined by floor(i/2)\n            \n            # For a given node X, we want to find all nodes at distance K\n            # We can do BFS but we need to avoid exploring the entire tree\n            \n            # Instead, we can use a level-by-level approach\n            # Starting from X, we explore K levels outward\n            \n            visited = set()\n            queue = deque([(X, 0)])  # (node, distance)\n            visited.add(X)\n            count = 0\n            \n            while queue:\n                node, dist = queue.popleft()\n                \n                if dist == K:\n                    count += 1\n                    continue\n                elif dist > K:\n                    break\n                \n                # Add parent\n                if node != 1:  # parent exists\n                    p = parent(node)\n                    if p not in visited:\n                        visited.add(p)\n                        queue.append((p, dist + 1))\n                \n                # Add children\n                for child in children(node):\n                    if child not in visited:\n                        visited.add(child)\n                        queue.append((child, dist + 1))\n            \n            results.append(str(count))\n    \n    print(\"\\n\".join(results))\n\nsolve()\n```",
