    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array nums and an integer target.\nA 0-indexed array infinite_nums is generated by infinitely appending the elements of nums to itself.\nReturn the length of the shortest subarray of the array infinite_nums with a sum equal to target. If there is no such subarray return -1.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3], target = 5\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [1,2,3,1,2,3,1,2,...].\nThe subarray in the range [1,2], has the sum equal to target = 5 and length = 2.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the shortest length of a subarray with sum equal to target = 5.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,1,1,2,3], target = 4\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [1,1,1,2,3,1,1,1,2,3,1,1,...].\nThe subarray in the range [4,5], has the sum equal to target = 4 and length = 2.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the shortest length of a subarray with sum equal to target = 4.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [2,4,6,8], target = 3\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [2,4,6,8,2,4,6,8,...].\nIt can be proven that there is no subarray with sum equal to target = 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5\n1 <= target <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the shortest subarray in an infinitely repeated array that sums to the target.\n\nKey insights:\n1. If the target is very large, the optimal solution might involve taking complete copies of the array plus a partial subarray\n2. I can use a sliding window approach on the doubled array (nums + nums) to handle the wrap-around cases\n3. I need to consider both cases: subarrays that don't wrap around and those that do\n\nLet me trace through the approach:\n- First, calculate the sum of the original array\n- If target is larger than the array sum, I'll need some complete copies plus a remainder\n- Use sliding window on nums + nums to find the shortest subarray that sums to the remainder\n- Handle the case where no solution exists\n\n```python\ndef minSizeSubarray(nums, target):\n    n = len(nums)\n    total_sum = sum(nums)\n    \n    # If target is larger than total_sum, we need complete copies\n    complete_copies = target // total_sum\n    remainder = target % total_sum\n    \n    # If remainder is 0, we just need complete_copies * n\n    if remainder == 0:\n        return complete_copies * n\n    \n    # Use sliding window on doubled array to find shortest subarray with sum = remainder\n    # We also need to check for subarrays with sum = remainder + total_sum (equivalent modulo total_sum)\n    min_length = float('inf')\n    \n    # Try to find subarray with sum exactly equal to remainder\n    left = 0\n    current_sum = 0\n    \n    for right in range(2 * n):\n        current_sum += nums[right % n]\n        \n        while current_sum > remainder and left <= right:\n            current_sum -= nums[left % n]\n            left += 1\n            \n        if current_sum == remainder:\n            min_length = min(min_length, right - left + 1)\n    \n    # If we couldn't find a subarray with sum = remainder, try sum = remainder + total_sum\n    # This handles cases where we need to \"wrap around\" and the sum appears as remainder + total_sum\n    if min_length == float('inf'):\n        left = 0\n        current_sum = 0\n        target_sum = remainder + total_sum\n        \n        for right in range(2 * n):\n            current_sum += nums[right % n]\n            \n            while current_sum > target_sum and left <= right:\n                current_sum -= nums[left % n]\n                left += 1\n                \n            if current_sum == target_sum:\n                min_length = min(min_length, right - left + 1)\n    \n    # If still not found, check if we can achieve the target with complete copies plus a subarray\n    # We need to find the shortest subarray that sums to remainder (or remainder + k*total_sum)\n    # But since we're working with positive numbers, the shortest will be with the smallest equivalent sum\n    if min_length == float('inf'):\n        return -1\n    \n    return complete_copies * n + min_length\n```",
