    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_495",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array nums and two integers cost1 and cost2. You are allowed to perform either of the following operations any number of times:\n\nChoose an index i from nums and increase nums[i] by 1 for a cost of cost1.\nChoose two different indices i, j, from nums and increase nums[i] and nums[j] by 1 for a cost of cost2.\n\nReturn the minimum cost required to make all elements in the array equal. \nSince the answer may be very large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [4,1], cost1 = 5, cost2 = 2\nOutput: 15\nExplanation: \nThe following operations can be performed to make the values equal:\n\nIncrease nums[1] by 1 for a cost of 5. nums becomes [4,2].\nIncrease nums[1] by 1 for a cost of 5. nums becomes [4,3].\nIncrease nums[1] by 1 for a cost of 5. nums becomes [4,4].\n\nThe total cost is 15.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,3,3,3,5], cost1 = 2, cost2 = 1\nOutput: 6\nExplanation: \nThe following operations can be performed to make the values equal:\n\nIncrease nums[0] and nums[1] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [3,4,3,3,5].\nIncrease nums[0] and nums[2] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [4,4,4,3,5].\nIncrease nums[0] and nums[3] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [5,4,4,4,5].\nIncrease nums[1] and nums[2] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [5,5,5,4,5].\nIncrease nums[3] by 1 for a cost of 2. nums becomes [5,5,5,5,5].\n\nThe total cost is 6.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [3,5,3], cost1 = 1, cost2 = 3\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nThe following operations can be performed to make the values equal:\n\nIncrease nums[0] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [4,5,3].\nIncrease nums[0] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [5,5,3].\nIncrease nums[2] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [5,5,4].\nIncrease nums[2] by 1 for a cost of 1. nums becomes [5,5,5].\n\nThe total cost is 4.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^6\n1 <= cost1 <= 10^6\n1 <= cost2 <= 10^6",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minCostToEqualize(nums, cost1, cost2):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # If cost2 >= 2*cost1, then it's never beneficial to use the second operation\n    # because doing two single increments would be cheaper\n    if cost2 >= 2 * cost1:\n        max_val = max(nums)\n        total_cost = 0\n        for num in nums:\n            total_cost += (max_val - num) * cost1\n            total_cost %= MOD\n        return total_cost\n    \n    # Otherwise, we want to make all elements equal to the maximum value\n    # because increasing values is the only allowed operation\n    max_val = max(nums)\n    \n    # Calculate the total increments needed for each position\n    increments = [max_val - num for num in nums]\n    total_increments = sum(increments)\n    \n    # Use operation 2 as much as possible (pairwise increments)\n    # We can perform operation 2 at most total_increments // 2 times\n    # But we're limited by the availability of pairs\n    \n    # The maximum number of operation 2 we can perform is limited by:\n    # 1. Total increments available: total_increments // 2\n    # 2. The constraint that we need pairs of elements that still need increments\n    \n    # We can model this as: use operation 2 as much as possible, then use operation 1 for the remainder\n    \n    # The optimal strategy is to use operation 2 as much as possible\n    # The maximum number of operation 2 is limited by the total_increments // 2\n    # But we also need to consider if we have enough pairs\n    \n    # Actually, since we can choose any two different indices, as long as at least\n    # two elements need increments, we can perform operation 2\n    # So the limiting factor is just total_increments // 2\n    \n    max_operation_2 = total_increments // 2\n    remaining_increments = total_increments - 2 * max_operation_2\n    \n    total_cost = max_operation_2 * cost2 + remaining_increments * cost1\n    \n    return total_cost % MOD\n```",
