      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    n = int(input())\n    intervals = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        l, r = map(int, input().split())\n        intervals.append((l, r))\n    \n    count = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            l1, r1 = intervals[i]\n            l2, r2 = intervals[j]\n            # Two intervals [l1, r1] and [l2, r2] intersect if max(l1, l2) < min(r1, r2)\n            # or if they touch at a point (if we consider touching as intersection)\n            # But based on the examples, [3,7] and [7,8] intersect, so touching at endpoint counts\n            # So they intersect if max(l1, l2) <= min(r1, r2)\n            if max(l1, l2) <= min(r1, r2):\n                count += 1\n    \n    print(count)\n\nsolve()\n```",
