      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the problem:\n\nWe are to find the **minimum integer X** such that:\n\n> The number of sellers who can sell an apple for **X yen or less** (i.e., their minimum price A_i ≤ X)  \n> is **greater than or equal to**  \n> the number of buyers who can buy an apple for **X yen or more** (i.e., their maximum price B_i ≥ X).\n\nWait — actually, let's read carefully:\n\n> The i-th seller may sell an apple for A_i yen **or more** → so they would **accept X** if **A_i ≤ X** (because they want at least A_i, so X ≥ A_i).\n> The i-th buyer may buy for B_i yen **or less** → so they would **accept X** if **B_i ≥ X** (because they pay at most B_i, so X ≤ B_i).\n\nSo at price X:\n\n- Number of **sellers willing to sell** = number of i such that **A_i ≤ X**\n- Number of **buyers willing to buy** = number of i such that **B_i ≥ X**\n\nWe want the **smallest X** such that:\n\n    count_sellers(X) >= count_buyers(X)\n\nThat is:\n\n    (# of A_i ≤ X) >= (# of B_i ≥ X)\n\n---\n\nWe cannot try all X from 1 to 10^9, but we can **binary search on X**.\n\n### Plan:\n- Sort the seller array A\n- Sort the buyer array B\n- Binary search on X in a suitable range (say from 1 to 10^9 + 10)\n- For a given X:\n  - Count how many A_i ≤ X → use binary search (bisect_right)\n  - Count how many B_i ≥ X → equivalent to M - bisect_left(B, X)\n- Find the **minimum X** such that count_A ≤ X >= count_B ≥ X\n\nLet’s implement accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport bisect\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    m = int(data[1])\n    A = list(map(int, data[2:2+n]))\n    B = list(map(int, data[2+n:2+n+m]))\n    \n    A.sort()\n    B.sort()\n    \n    # Binary search on X\n    lo = 1\n    hi = 10**9 + 10\n    ans = hi\n    \n    while lo <= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        # Count sellers with A_i <= mid\n        sellers = bisect.bisect_right(A, mid)\n        # Count buyers with B_i >= mid\n        buyers = m - bisect.bisect_left(B, mid)\n        \n        if sellers >= buyers:\n            ans = mid\n            hi = mid - 1\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n            \n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
