    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numberOfWays(s: str, t: str, k: int) -> int:\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Find all valid shifts that transform s to t\n    valid_shifts = []\n    s_doubled = s + s\n    \n    # Check each possible shift\n    for shift in range(1, n):\n        if s_doubled[shift:shift + n] == t:\n            valid_shifts.append(shift)\n    \n    if not valid_shifts:\n        return 0\n    \n    m = len(valid_shifts)\n    \n    # For large k, we need to use matrix exponentiation concepts\n    # We can model this as a recurrence relation\n    # Let dp[i] = number of ways to reach any valid shift in i operations\n    # We have two cases:\n    # 1. Coming from a valid shift position\n    # 2. Coming from an invalid shift position\n    \n    # Using mathematical derivation:\n    # If we let f(k) = number of ways after k operations\n    # We can derive that:\n    # f(k) = (m * ((n-1)**(k-1) - (-1)**(k-1)) // n) % MOD if k >= 1\n    \n    # But we need to handle this with modular arithmetic and large exponents\n    \n    if k == 1:\n        return m % MOD\n    \n    # Use the recurrence relation with matrix exponentiation pattern\n    # The recurrence can be optimized using the fact that:\n    # ways(k) = (m * (power(n-1, k-1) - power(-1, k-1))) / n\n    \n    def mod_pow(base, exp, mod):\n        result = 1\n        base = base % mod\n        while exp > 0:\n            if exp % 2 == 1:\n                result = (result * base) % mod\n            exp = exp >> 1\n            base = (base * base) % mod\n        return result\n    \n    # Calculate (n-1)^(k-1) mod MOD\n    term1 = mod_pow(n - 1, k - 1, MOD)\n    \n    # Calculate (-1)^(k-1) mod MOD\n    term2 = 1 if (k - 1) % 2 == 0 else MOD - 1  # (-1)^(k-1) mod MOD\n    \n    # Calculate m * (term1 - term2) * mod_inverse(n, MOD) mod MOD\n    diff = (term1 - term2 + MOD) % MOD\n    result = (m * diff) % MOD\n    \n    # Calculate modular inverse of n\n    def mod_inverse(a, mod):\n        return mod_pow(a, mod - 2, mod)\n    \n    inv_n = mod_inverse(n, MOD)\n    result = (result * inv_n) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
