    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minOperations(nums):\n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    # Count frequency of each number\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    \n    operations = 0\n    \n    for count in freq.values():\n        # If any number appears only once, it's impossible to remove it\n        if count == 1:\n            return -1\n        \n        # For each group of same numbers, calculate minimum operations\n        # We want to maximize the use of operation 2 (remove 3) since it's more efficient\n        # Any count can be expressed as 3k, 3k+1, or 3k+2\n        if count % 3 == 0:\n            # Perfectly divisible by 3\n            operations += count // 3\n        elif count % 3 == 1:\n            # Count is of form 3k+1, e.g., 4, 7, 10...\n            # We need to use one operation of type 1 and (k-1) operations of type 2\n            # For example, 4 = 2 + 2 (two operations of type 1), or better: 4 = 3 + 1 (but 1 can't be removed)\n            # Actually, 4 = 2 + 2, so we need 2 operations of type 1\n            # For 7: 7 = 3 + 2 + 2, so one operation of type 2 and two of type 1 = 3 operations\n            # In general: 3k+1 = 3*(k-1) + 2*2, so (k-1) + 2 = k+1 operations\n            operations += (count // 3 - 1) + 2\n        else:  # count % 3 == 2\n            # Count is of form 3k+2, e.g., 2, 5, 8...\n            # We use k operations of type 2 and 1 operation of type 1\n            operations += count // 3 + 1\n    \n    return operations\n```",
