      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of ways to split a binary array into good subarrays, where each good subarray contains exactly one element with value 1.\n\nLet me analyze the pattern:\n- Each subarray must contain exactly one 1\n- So if there are k ones in the array, I need exactly k subarrays\n- The splits can only happen between consecutive ones\n- Between two consecutive ones, I can choose where to split (after any of the zeros between them)\n\nFor example, with nums = [0,1,0,0,1]:\n- First 1 is at index 1, second 1 is at index 4\n- Between them are 2 zeros (at indices 2,3)\n- I can split after the first 1, which means the split can be after position 1, 2, or 3\n- This gives me 3 choices (number of zeros between + 1)\n\nThe general approach:\n1. Find all positions of 1s\n2. If there are no 1s, return 0 (impossible to have good subarrays)\n3. If there's only one 1, there's exactly one way to split (the entire array)\n4. For multiple 1s, the number of ways is the product of (distance between consecutive 1s) for all consecutive pairs\n\n```python\ndef numberOfGoodSubarraySplits(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Find positions of all 1s\n    ones_positions = []\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        if nums[i] == 1:\n            ones_positions.append(i)\n    \n    # If no 1s, impossible to have good subarrays\n    if not ones_positions:\n        return 0\n    \n    # If only one 1, only one way to split\n    if len(ones_positions) == 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    # For multiple 1s, multiply the gaps between consecutive 1s\n    result = 1\n    for i in range(1, len(ones_positions)):\n        # Number of positions where we can split between ones_positions[i-1] and ones_positions[i]\n        gap = ones_positions[i] - ones_positions[i-1]\n        result = (result * gap) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
