      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}\n\nThe Supreme Court in *C&A Carbone, Inc. v. Town of Clarkstown* held that a flow control ordinance requiring all solid waste to be processed at a local facility discriminates against interstate commerce by economically protecting local processors, even if it applies equally to all waste within the town. Options asserting otherwise (like D or H) are incorrect because the ordinance's practical effect excludes out-of-state processors, violating the dormant Commerce Clause. Thus, the federal court would likely rule it discriminatory.",
