    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given a torus $ T $ formed by revolving a circle of radius $ 3 $ around an axis $ 6 $ units from its center, so the major radius is $ R = 6 $, and the minor radius is $ r = 3 $. When this torus rests on the outside of a sphere of radius $ 11 $, we consider two configurations: one where the torus wraps around the sphere and touches it externally along an inner circle (smaller radius $ r_i $), and one where it sits on the outside (larger radius $ r_o $). Using geometry of tangent surfaces of revolution, the radii of the tangent circles are found by considering cross-sections: $ r_i = \\frac{3 \\cdot 11}{6 + 3} = \\frac{33}{9} = \\frac{11}{3} $, and $ r_o = \\frac{3 \\cdot 11}{6 - 3} = \\frac{33}{3} = 11 $. But this reasoning is flawed—let’s correct it.\n\nActually, the correct approach uses similar triangles and the fact that the torus and sphere are tangent along a circle whose radius depends on the distance from the center of the sphere to the center of the generating circle of the torus. When the torus is externally tangent to the sphere, the center of the generating circle is at distance $ 11 + 3 = 14 $ from the sphere's center for the outer case, and $ 11 - 3 = 8 $ for the inner case. Then the radius of the circle of tangency is $ r_o = \\frac{6 \\cdot 11}{14} = \\frac{66}{14} = \\frac{33}{7} $, and $ r_i = \\frac{6 \\cdot 11}{8} = \\frac{66}{8} = \\frac{33}{4} $. But this contradicts intuition.\n\nWait — correct method: The torus has major radius $ R = 6 $, minor radius $ r = 3 $. When it rests on a sphere of radius $ S = 11 $, externally tangent, the center of the tube is at distance $ D $ from the sphere's center. For external tangency, $ D = 11 + 3 = 14 $, and the radius of the circle of tangency is $ r_o = \\frac{6 \\cdot 11}{14} = \\frac{66}{14} = \\frac{33}{7} $. For internal (torus surrounding the sphere), $ D = 11 - 3 = 8 $, so $ r_i = \\frac{6 \\cdot 11}{8} = \\frac{66}{8} = \\frac{33}{4} $. But then $ r_i > r_o $, so $ r_i - r_o = \\frac{33}{4} - \\frac{33}{7} = 33\\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{7}\\right) = 33 \\cdot \\frac{3}{28} = \\frac{99}{28} $. Thus $ m = 99, n = 28 $, relatively prime, so $ m + n = \\boxed{127} $.",
